---
/**
 * The four constructions side by side, and the questions that decide which one is yours.
 *
 * The comparison row is the load-bearing part of the table: its four cells answer four
 * different questions and are measured against four different yardsticks, so the
 * paragraph under the table says in words that they may never be added. That is the
 * error this project has made against itself more than once.
 *
 * Numbers in the table are printed as the draft states them, intervals included. The
 * `<Figure>` records are used in the prose, where a record's rendered value is a whole
 * phrase rather than a fragment of a sentence-length cell.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

/**
 * The four columns, in the order the site argues them.
 *
 * A column heading is the number and the name together. Below 40rem the table becomes
 * one card per attribute and each cell shows its column's `number` alone, because the
 * second of these headings runs to a hundred characters and a card label that long
 * buries the value beside it. Both forms are built from the same two fields here, so
 * they cannot drift apart.
 */
const PORTFOLIOS = [
  { number: "1", name: "The whole market", href: "/portfolios/whole-market/" },
  {
    number: "2",
    name: "The same fund, held in the cheapest form, in the account where it is taxed least, and never traded",
    href: "/portfolios/held-well/",
  },
  {
    number: "3",
    name: "A lean toward cheaper, smaller, more profitable companies",
    href: "/portfolios/value-lean/",
  },
  { number: "4", name: "The same, plus a holding that does not move with stocks", href: "/portfolios/with-trend/" },
] as const;

/** One row of the comparison: the attribute, then its value for each of the four. */
interface Row {
  readonly label: string;
  readonly cells: readonly [string, string, string, string];
}

const ROWS = [
  {
    label: "Holdings",
    cells: [
      "VT 100%",
      "VT 100%",
      "VTI 49%, VXUS 16%, VTV 15%, AVDV 10%, IDMO 5%, AVES 5%",
      "RSST 30%, VTI 19%, VXUS 16%, VTV 15%, AVDV 10%, IDMO 5%, AVES 5%",
    ],
  },
  { label: "Fees a year", cells: ["0.06%", "0.06%", "0.09%", "0.38%"] },
  { label: "On $10,000, as an illustration", cells: ["$6", "$6", "$9", "$38"] },
  {
    label: "Compared against",
    cells: [
      "Active funds trying to beat the same index",
      "Whatever you would otherwise have held",
      "A cheap global index fund",
      "A cheap global index fund borrowed to the same total exposure",
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "What the comparison showed",
    cells: [
      "90.3% of US stock funds lost to their benchmark over ten years, 93.8% over twenty",
      "Between about 0.04% and about 2.70% a year, depending entirely on what you hold now",
      "0.79% a year ahead, plausibly 0.30% to 1.32%",
      "2.20 points a year ahead, plausibly 0.05 to 4.57, against a test that could only see 2.83",
    ],
  },
  { label: "How sure we are", cells: ["Settled", "Settled", "Probably", "We can’t tell"] },
  {
    label: "The worst thing that has happened",
    cells: [
      "US stocks have fallen 83.7% from a peak. A cheap index that borrows nothing fell 52.7% in the window tested",
      "The same, because it holds the same fund",
      "Cheap US stocks fell 54.3% behind the US market over 17.7 years and have not caught up",
      "The tested version fell 50.3% and spent 42 months under water. A borrowed trend holding has run 59.9% behind the stocks it displaces for 11.2 years",
    ],
  },
] as const satisfies readonly Row[];
---

<Base
  title="The four portfolios"
  description="All four side by side, with what each costs, what it was compared against, and the worst thing that has happened to it."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">The four portfolios</h1>

    <p class="lead">Which of these to hold, and how to tell which one is yours. Each portfolio contains the one
    above it. You are choosing where to stop.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout scroll class="mt-10" heading="Side by side" maxWidth="62rem">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base sm:min-w-[58rem]">
      <caption class="sr-only">
        The four portfolios compared on holdings, fees, benchmark, evidence and worst outcome
      </caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left align-bottom">
          <td role="cell" class="py-2 pr-4"></td>
          {
            PORTFOLIOS.map((portfolio) => (
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4">
                <a href={portfolio.href} class="link">{`${portfolio.number}. ${portfolio.name}`}</a>
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-muted">
        {
          ROWS.map((row) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule align-top last:border-0">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-medium text-ink">
                {row.label}
              </th>
              {row.cells.map((cell, i) => (
                <td role="cell" data-label={PORTFOLIOS[i]?.number} class="py-2 pr-4">
                  {cell}
                </td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-8">
    <p>Portfolios one and two hold the same fund. What separates them is how it is held, and how much that is
    worth depends on what you hold now. The four numbers in the comparison row answer four different questions
    and must never be added together. Being better off than the funds you used to own says nothing about beating
    an index, and beating an index says nothing about either.</p>

    <p>Fees come from each fund&rsquo;s own filing, read on 17 August 2026, weighted by the holdings shown. The
    rest of the figures were measured on 427 months of data ending May 2026, except the survivorship figures,
    which are S&amp;P&rsquo;s mid-2025 scorecard.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>The decision that is bigger than all of this</h2>

    <p>How much of your money sits in stocks at all is not on this page, and it outweighs everything that is.
    Moving a portfolio from 60% stocks to 90% stocks was worth 1.27 points a year, against 4.85 points of
    variation between one year and the next. That is larger than every lean on this site put together. Choosing
    60/40 instead is itself a forecast: it says stocks will do better than bonds by only about 1.2 points a
    year.</p>

    <p>That measurement covers one country, 1963 to 2025, with a modelled ten-year government bond. It would take
    about <Figure id="equity-step-horizon" inline /> of your own results to be 90% confident the step was worth
    taking.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>How to choose</h2>

    <p>Nobody here knows your situation, so these are questions rather than instructions.</p>

    <p>Have you ever held something that lost money for ten years running, and not sold it? If the honest answer
    is that you have never been tested, stop at <a href="/portfolios/held-well/">portfolio two</a>. Intention is
    not evidence about yourself.</p>

    <p>Do you already hold cheap index funds, in sensible accounts, and rarely trade? Then
    <a href="/portfolios/held-well/">portfolio two</a> is worth nothing to you, because you are already doing it.
    The saving is measured against what you would otherwise have held, and for you that is the same thing.</p>

    <p>Would you rather own one fund or six? Six holdings mean six things to
    <a href="/evidence/rebalancing/">rebalance</a> and six chances to fiddle. An annual review with a wide band
    keeps a six-fund portfolio about 0.94 points from its targets, against 6.39 points if it is left entirely
    alone, and costs roughly three trades a year.</p>

    <p>Would a bad decade change any plan you have? If money is coming out of the portfolio inside ten years, the
    question of how much sits in stocks matters far more than which of these four you pick.</p>

    <p>Are you comfortable that one fund from a small firm carries
    <Figure id="active-variance-concentration" inline showUnit={false} /> of
    <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">portfolio four</a>&rsquo;s active risk? If that sentence makes you uneasy,
    that is the correct reaction, and <a href="/portfolios/value-lean/">portfolio three</a> is where to stop.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What each page contains</h2>

    <p>Each portfolio has its own page: what it holds, why each piece is there, what it was compared against, how
    sure we are in one word, what it feels like when it is losing, and what would make us drop it.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
